            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTRTX_EP1_TX</name>
              <description>
              Tx Endpoint 1 interrupt.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INTRTX_EP0</name>
              <description>
              Endpoint 0 interrupt
              </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_ISO_UPDATE</name>
              <description>
              When set by the CPU, the MUSBHDRC will wait for an SOF token from the time TxPktRdy is set
              before sending the packet. If an IN token is received before an SOF token, then a zero length data
              packet will be sent. Note: Only valid in Peripheral Mode. Also, this bit only affects endpoints performing Isochronous
              transfers.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_SOFT_CONN</name>
              <description>
              If Soft Connect/Disconnect feature is enabled, then the USB D+/D- lines are enabled when this bit
              is set by the CPU and tri-stated when this bit is cleared by the CPU. (See Section 5.1) Note: Only valid in
              Peripheral Mode.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_HS_ENAB</name>
              <description>
              When set by the CPU, the MUSBHDRC will negotiate for High-speed mode when the device is reset
              by the hub. If not set, the device will only operate in Full-speed mode.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_HS_MODE</name>
              <description>
              When set, this read-only bit indicates High-speed mode successfully negotiated during USB reset.
              In Peripheral Mode, becomes valid when USB reset completes (as indicated by USB reset interrupt).
              In Host Mode, becomes valid when Reset bit is cleared. Remains valid for the duration of the session.
              Note: Allowance is made for Tiny-J signaling in determining the transfer speed to select.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_RESET</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set when Reset signaling is present on the bus. Note: This bit is Read/Write from the CPU in
              Host Mode but Read-Only in Peripheral Mode.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>11</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_RESUME</name>
              <description>
              Set by the CPU to generate Resume signaling when the function is in Suspend mode. The CPU should clear
              this bit after 10 ms (a maximum of 15 ms) to end Resume signaling. In Host mode, this bit is also
              automatically set when Resume signaling from the target is detected while the MUSBHDRC is suspended.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_SUSPEND_MODE</name>
              <description>
              In Host mode, this bit is set by the CPU to enter Suspend mode. In Peripheral mode, this bit is set on entry
              into Suspend mode. It is cleared when the CPU reads the interrupt register, or sets the Resume bit above.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POWER_ENABLE_SUSPENDM</name>
              <description>
              Set by the CPU to enable the SUSPENDM signal.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>
              </description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FADDR_FUNC_ADDR</name>
              <description>
              The function address.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
