              <description>
              The CPU sets this bit to enable the DMA request for the Rx endpoint.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_DISNYET_PIDERR</name>
              <description>
              Bulk/Interrupt Transactions: The CPU sets this bit to disable the sending of NYET handshakes.
              When set, all successfully received Rx packets are ACK'd including at the point at which the FIFO
              becomes full. Note: This bit only has any effect in High-speed mode, in which mode it should be set for all Interrupt endpoints.
              ISO Transactions: The core sets this bit to indicate a PID error in the received packet.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_DMAREQMODE</name>
              <description>
              The CPU sets this bit to select DMA Mode 1 and clears this bit to select DMA Mode 0.
              Note: This bit should not be cleared in the same cycle as RxPktRdy is cleared.
              endpoints that are used to communicate rate feedback for Isochronous endpoints.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>
              </description>
              <bitOffset>25</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_INCOMPRX</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set in a high-bandwidth Isochronous/Interrupt transfer if the packet in the Rx FIFO is
              incomplete because parts of the data were not received. It is cleared when RxPktRdy is cleared. Note:
              In anything other than a high-bandwidth transfer, this bit will always return 0.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_CLRDATATOG</name>
              <description>
              The CPU writes a 1 to this bit to reset the endpoint data toggle to 0.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>23</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_SENTSTALL</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set when a STALL handshake is transmitted. The FIFO is flushed and the TxPktRdy bit
              is cleared (see below). The CPU should clear this bit.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_SENDSTALL</name>
              <description>
              The CPU writes a 1 to this bit to issue a STALL handshake to an IN token. The CPU clears this
              bit to terminate the stall condition. Note::(i) The FIFO should be flushed before SendStall is set. (ii) This bit has no effect where the endpoint is being used for Isochronous transfers.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>21</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_FLUSHFIFO</name>
              <description>
              The CPU writes a 1 to this bit to flush the next packet to be read from the endpoint Rx FIFO.
              The FIFO pointer is reset and the RxPktRdy bit (below) is cleared. Note: FlushFIFO should only be
              used when RxPktRdy is set. At other times, it may cause data to be corrupted. Also note that, if the
              FIFO is double-buffered, FlushFIFO may need to be set twice to completely clear the FIFO..
              </description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_DATAERROR</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set when RxPktRdy is set if the data packet has a CRC or bit-stuff error. It is cleared when RxPktRdy is cleared. Note: This bit is only valid when the endpoint is operating in ISO mode. In Bulk mode, it always returns zero.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_OVERRUN</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set if an OUT packet cannot be loaded into the Rx FIFO. The CPU should clear this bit.
              Note: This bit is only valid when the endpoint is operating in ISO mode. In Bulk mode, it always returns zero.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_FIFOFULL</name>
              <description>
              This bit is set when no more packets can be loaded into the Rx FIFO.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCSR_RXPKTRDY</name>
              <description>
              CThis bit is set when a data packet has been received. The CPU should clear this bit when the packet
              has been unloaded from the Rx FIFO. An interrupt is generated when the bit is set.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXMAXP_RXMAXP</name>
              <description>
              Maximum packet size for peripheral Rx endpoint. (Index register set to select Endpoints
              1 – 15 only)
              </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RXCOUNT_TXTYPE_1</name>
          <displayName>RXCOUNT_TXTYPE_1</displayName>
          <description>RXCOUNT_TXType</description>
          <addressOffset>0x118</addressOffset>
          <size>0x20</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>
              </description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXTYPE_PROTOCOL</name>
              <description>
              The CPU should set this to select the required protocol for the Tx endpoint: 
               00: Illegal 
               01: Isochronous 
               10: Bulk 
               11: Interrupt
              </description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TXTYPE_TARGET_EP</name>
              <description>
              The CPU should set this value to the endpoint number contained in the Tx endpoint descriptor 
              </description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD2</name>
              <description>
              returned to the MUSBHDRC during device enumeration. 
              </description>
              <bitOffset>13</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXCOUNT</name>
              <description>
              RxCount is a 13-bit read-only register that holds the number of received data bytes in the packet currently in line to be read from the Rx FIFO. If the packet was transmitted as multiple bulk packets, the number given will be for the combined packet. 
              Note: The value returned changes as the FIFO is unloaded and is only valid while RxPktRdy (RxCSR.D0) is set.
              </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>13</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RXTYPE_FIFOSIZE_1</name>
          <displayName>RXTYPE_FIFOSIZE_1</displayName>
          <description>RXType_Fifo Size </description>
          <addressOffset>0x11c</addressOffset>
          <size>0x20</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FIFOSIZE_RX</name>
              <description>
              FIFOSize is an 8-bit Read-Only register that returns the sizes of the FIFOs associated with the selected additional Tx/Rx endpoints. The lower nibble encodes the size of the selected Tx endpoint FIFO; the upper nibble encodes the size of the selected Rx endpoint FIFO. Values of 3 – 13 correspond to a FIFO size of 2n bytes (8 – 8192 bytes). If an endpoint has not been configured, a value of 0 will be displayed. Where the Tx and Rx endpoints share the same FIFO, the Rx FIFO size will be encoded as 0xF
              </description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIFOSIZE_TX</name>
              <description>
              FIFOSize is an 8-bit Read-Only register that returns the sizes of the FIFOs associated with the selected additional Tx/Rx endpoints. The lower nibble encodes the size of the selected Tx endpoint FIFO; the upper nibble encodes the size of the selected Rx endpoint FIFO. Values of 3 – 13 correspond to a FIFO size of 2n bytes (8 – 8192 bytes). If an endpoint has not been configured, a value of 0 will be displayed. Where the Tx and Rx endpoints share the same FIFO, the Rx FIFO size will be encoded as 0xF
              </description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>
              </description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>18</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTYPE_PROTOCOL</name>
              <description>
              The CPU should set this to select the required protocol for the Rx endpoint: 
               00: Illegal 
               01: Isochronous 
               10: Bulk 
               11: Interrupt
              </description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RXTYPE_TARGET_EP</name>
              <description>
              The CPU should set this value to the endpoint number contained in the Rx endpoint descriptor 
              </description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
